<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  macOS entitlements.

  This file deliberately requests NOTHING, and that is the point: every
  entitlement weakens the hardened runtime, and each one has to be justified by
  something the app actually cannot do without it. Notarization requires the
  hardened runtime, which Tauri enables when signing; it does not require any
  entitlement here.

  Considered and rejected:

  * `com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit` — the web view's JIT runs inside Apple's
    own `com.apple.WebKit.WebContent` process, which carries its own
    entitlements. The host process does not compile JavaScript.
  * `com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation` — nothing is dlopen'd at
    runtime; the Rust binary links the system frameworks and is otherwise
    static. `mysqldump` and `pg_dump` are separate processes with their own
    signatures, not libraries loaded into this one.
  * App Sandbox entitlements — this app is distributed with Developer ID, not
    through the App Store. Sandboxing it would break the two things it exists
    to do: run `mysqldump`/`pg_dump` from wherever the user installed them, and
    write backups to a directory the user chooses.

  A NOTE ON THE KEYCHAIN, which is the one thing that genuinely surprises
  people here: keychain items are bound to the code signature that created
  them. Re-signing this app with a different identity — going from an ad-hoc
  local build to a Developer ID build, or rotating certificates — makes macOS
  treat it as a different application, and previously saved database passwords
  will prompt for permission again. That is macOS working correctly, not a bug
  in the app. Users re-grant access once; nothing is lost.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
</dict>
</plist>
